1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,975
Chapter 3: User Memory and Knowledge Base.

2
00:00:03,925 --> 00:00:08,887
The previous chapter addressed context management within a single interaction.

3
00:00:08,887 --> 00:00:17,350
This chapter tackles a more difficult problem: how to enable an Agent to remember users and retain knowledge even after a conversation ends.

4
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:21,712
This persistent memory system can be understood at two scales.

5
00:00:21,712 --> 00:00:33,787
User Memory is personalized memory for an individual user—the Agent gradually learns each user's preferences, habits, and needs through interactions, building a knowledge model unique to that user.

6
00:00:33,787 --> 00:00:46,050
Knowledge Base is collective knowledge shared across all users—such as an industry's regulatory framework, a company's internal operating procedures, or specialized technical documentation in a field.

7
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:53,212
The former makes the Agent a "personal assistant who knows you," while the latter makes the Agent a "domain expert.

8
00:00:53,212 --> 00:00:59,912
The two are really the same problem at different scales—one centered on the individual, the other on the group.

9
00:00:59,912 --> 00:01:12,375
That is why they share so much underlying technology (vector retrieval, knowledge compression) and encounter the same failure modes: conflicting information, stale knowledge, and inaccurate retrieval.

10
00:01:12,375 --> 00:01:22,862
Continuing the context engineering approach from Chapter 2, this chapter extends context management from single-session conversations to a cross-session persistent knowledge system.

11
00:01:22,862 --> 00:01:33,412
We first explore how to build a user memory system, then delve into Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) for knowledge bases and how it enhances user memory.

12
00:01:33,412 --> 00:01:38,087
As illustrated in Figure 3-1: Chapter Knowledge Map.

13
00:01:38,087 --> 00:01:40,312
User Memory System.

14
00:01:40,312 --> 00:01:46,675
To offer personalized service across sessions, an Agent needs a layer of persistent user memory.

15
00:01:46,675 --> 00:02:00,100
It does not store every utterance; instead it uses an extra LLM call to extract, compress, and vet the facts that will be useful later—unlike in-context learning, which only takes effect within the current window.

16
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:03,487
A concrete example makes the process clear.

17
00:02:03,487 --> 00:02:07,587
Suppose a user and an Agent have the following exchange:

18
00:02:07,587 --> 00:02:12,325
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

19
00:02:12,325 --> 00:02:21,450
Once the conversation ends, the Agent framework makes one dedicated LLM call to analyze it and extract what is worth remembering long term:

20
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:26,187
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

21
00:02:26,187 --> 00:02:43,037
The extraction should satisfy three rules at once: selectivity (discard short-lived detail such as "the search returned 3 options"), abstraction (generalize this one "window seat" into a lasting preference), and structure (store facts in retrievable fields).

22
00:02:43,037 --> 00:02:47,462
Evaluating Memory Capabilities: A Three-Level Framework.

23
00:02:47,462 --> 00:02:53,887
Before designing a memory system, first answer one question: what makes a memory system "good"?

24
00:02:53,887 --> 00:03:00,287
Setting the evaluation criteria up front gives us a common yardstick for every design discussed later.

25
00:03:00,287 --> 00:03:07,575
Several public benchmarks exist; a representative one is LoCoMo (Long-term Conversational Memory).

26
00:03:07,575 --> 00:03:30,662
It constructs ultra-long dialogues averaging about 300 turns across up to 35 sessions, and probes a model's memory and understanding of long-range conversation through three task families: question answering (subdivided into single-hop, multi-hop, temporal reasoning, open-domain, and adversarial questions), event summarization, and multimodal dialogue generation.

27
00:03:30,662 --> 00:03:44,200
Drawing on LoCoMo and its peers, together with the practice of commercial memory products, user memory capabilities can be distilled into eight categories (the author's synthesis, not any single benchmark's original taxonomy

28
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:50,250
Personal Information Retention: Remembering long-term personal information like user identity

29
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:55,425
Preference Tracking: Tracking and remembering the user's long-term preferences

30
00:03:55,425 --> 00:04:00,912
Context Switching: Maintaining coherence when switching between multiple topics

31
00:04:00,912 --> 00:04:06,425
Memory Update: Correctly handling new information that contradicts old information

32
00:04:06,425 --> 00:04:11,012
Multi-Session Continuity: Maintaining knowledge across sessions

33
00:04:11,012 --> 00:04:22,037
Complex Reasoning: Reasoning across multiple memory fragments, e.g., proactively reminding a user with a peanut allergy to watch for peanut ingredients when recommending Thai cuisine

34
00:04:22,204 --> 00:04:29,141
Temporal Awareness: Remembering dates, understanding relative time, performing time calculations

35
00:04:29,091 --> 00:04:34,779
Conflict Resolution: Identifying and handling inconsistencies between memories

36
00:04:34,779 --> 00:04:44,491
Building on this, we designed a three-level evaluation framework more tailored to Agent scenarios, decomposing memory capabilities into progressive levels.

37
00:04:44,491 --> 00:04:54,654
This framework recurs throughout this chapter—Experiments 3-9 and 3-11 later will use it to measure how retrieval techniques improve memory capabilities.

38
00:04:54,654 --> 00:05:07,641
Level 1: Basic Recall — This is the most fundamental capability of a memory system, requiring the Agent to accurately store and retrieve information that the user provides directly and that is structured and unambiguous.

39
00:05:07,641 --> 00:05:15,191
For example, "My membership number is 12345" should be precisely returned when needed later.

40
00:05:15,191 --> 00:05:22,791
This level ensures the basic reliability of the memory system and serves as the foundation for more complex capabilities.

41
00:05:22,791 --> 00:05:37,691
Level 2: Multi-Session Retrieval — The Agent must retrieve and reason over all relevant information when conversations span different entities, service channels, and time periods; real-world tasks are rarely completed in a single conversation.

42
00:05:37,691 --> 00:05:47,016
When a user with two cars asks "Schedule maintenance for my car," the system needs to find both cars and ask which one needs service, not guess.

43
00:05:47,016 --> 00:05:56,066
When the user asks about loan status, it must pick out the active contract currently in force and ignore past quote inquiries that never took effect.

44
00:05:56,066 --> 00:06:06,004
When canceling a "Los Angeles trip," it must understand that a trip is a composite event and proactively link every related booking—flights and hotels alike.

45
00:06:06,004 --> 00:06:22,916
Level 3: Proactive Service — This is the acid test of whether an Agent has truly reached assistant-level capability: synthesizing information across many sessions, some of them very old, to offer predictive help—finding deep connections between memories that look unrelated.

46
00:06:22,916 --> 00:06:32,016
When the user books an international flight, the system surfaces the passport stored months ago, notices it is about to expire, and warns them.

47
00:06:32,016 --> 00:06:43,404
When a phone breaks, it pulls together every protection option—the phone's own warranty, the credit card's extended-warranty terms, the carrier's insurance—into one complete list.

48
00:06:43,404 --> 00:06:54,091
During tax season, it combs the past year's records for every tax document (stock sales, freelance income, property taxes) and presents a full to-do list.

49
00:06:54,091 --> 00:06:59,979
All of this means heading off problems and integrating complex information without being asked.

50
00:06:59,979 --> 00:07:07,791
Experiment 3-1 introductory difficulty, one star: : Evaluating Memory Systems with the Three-Level Framework

51
00:07:07,791 --> 00:07:16,679
We built an evaluation set following the three-level framework above: 20 test cases per level, each containing a wealth of factual details.

52
00:07:16,679 --> 00:07:28,929
Level 1 cases typically consist of a single session; Level 2 and 3 cases consist of multiple sessions across different times and entities (approximately 50 total communication turns per case).

53
00:07:28,929 --> 00:07:44,854
During evaluation, the Agent under test is required to generate memories based on the first session, then modify memories based on subsequent sessions (with access only to the memory, not the original conversation history), until all sessions for that case are processed.

54
00:07:44,854 --> 00:07:50,929
After memory generation, the Agent is asked to answer a new user question based on the memory.

55
00:07:50,929 --> 00:08:02,529
An LLM-as-a-judge method (using another LLM as a judge to score answer quality) is then used to compare the answer against a reference answer, yielding a reward score for that test case.

56
00:08:02,529 --> 00:08:09,529
This evaluation set and evaluation script are included in the user-memory project of the companion repository.

57
00:08:09,529 --> 00:08:14,516
Readers can view the complete definitions of test cases for each level there.

58
00:08:14,516 --> 00:08:17,429
The Hierarchical Structure of Memory.

59
00:08:17,429 --> 00:08:22,666
With evaluation criteria established, we can move to concrete design.

60
00:08:22,666 --> 00:08:31,316
The design of a memory system can be broken down into three independent dimensions—where to store it, how to store it, and what to store.

61
00:08:31,316 --> 00:08:34,454
This section addresses "where to store it.

62
00:08:34,612 --> 00:08:48,437
To enable the Agent to efficiently handle current tasks while providing personalized service across sessions, memory needs to be divided into different levels—much like humans distinguish between short-term working memory and long-term memory:

63
00:08:48,387 --> 00:09:02,637
Trajectory is the complete historical record of a single Agent run—corresponding to the "dynamic trajectory" defined in Chapter 1 (user messages + model replies + tool execution results, collectively called the trajectory).

64
00:09:02,637 --> 00:09:18,274
The trajectory records every event from the start of the conversation to the current moment, in chronological order and never rewritten—new events keep getting appended to the end, but records once written are never modified or deleted (the pattern computer science calls append-only).

65
00:09:18,274 --> 00:09:25,149
Here, "append-only" describes the original event records used for tracing, debugging, or auditing.

66
00:09:25,149 --> 00:09:40,574
The runtime Context actually sent to the model on each turn may be compressed or reorganized to control its length, or may replace part of the history with a summary; whether the original records are retained in full depends on the specific system's data-retention and audit requirements.

67
00:09:40,574 --> 00:09:49,599
The trajectory provides immediate context for Agent decision-making—"what did I just say," "how did the user respond," "what did the tool return.

68
00:09:49,599 --> 00:10:04,799
The trajectory is the complete raw record of a single session, appended chronologically and never modified; user long-term memory, on the other hand, is stable information distilled across sessions, which is repeatedly rewritten, merged, and pruned.

69
00:10:04,799 --> 00:10:08,549
The former is a log, the latter is an archive.

70
00:10:08,549 --> 00:10:17,587
User Long-Term Memory is persistent storage across sessions and instances, typically bound to a specific user ID via key-value pairs.

71
00:10:17,587 --> 00:10:23,612
It stores preference settings, historical interaction summaries, and extracted facts.

72
00:10:23,612 --> 00:10:32,637
The Agent explicitly reads and updates long-term memory through specific tool calls, enabling cross-session personalization and continuity.

73
00:10:32,637 --> 00:10:47,524
Additionally, some Agents support Business State—high-level state abstractions defined by developers, representing the logical stage of a task (e.g., "needs clarification," "processing request," "awaiting payment," "request completed").

74
00:10:47,524 --> 00:10:56,587
This type of state abstraction is particularly important in event-driven Agent architectures (Chapter 6 will discuss event-driven architecture design).

75
00:10:56,587 --> 00:11:02,487
This chapter focuses on the two core levels: trajectory and user long-term memory.

76
00:11:02,487 --> 00:11:13,687
The layered design ensures the Agent can efficiently handle current tasks (relying on trajectory) while possessing long-term personalization capabilities (relying on long-term memory).

77
00:11:13,687 --> 00:11:16,762
Four Storage Formats for User Memory.

78
00:11:16,762 --> 00:11:28,599
Having addressed "where to store it" and "how to evaluate it," the next question is "how to store it"—the same piece of user information can be represented with different granularities and structures.

79
00:11:28,599 --> 00:11:35,212
The following four storage formats represent a progression in memory granularity and structural complexity.

80
00:11:35,212 --> 00:11:41,049
As illustrated in Figure 3-2: Comparison of Four Memory Strategies.

81
00:11:41,049 --> 00:11:44,399
Simple Notes embodies a minimalist design.

82
00:11:44,399 --> 00:11:51,449
Each memory is a minimal, indivisible fact (e.g., "User email: john@example.com").

83
00:11:51,449 --> 00:11:59,099
The advantage is minimal overhead: O(1) operations (constant time, independent of data volume).

84
00:11:59,099 --> 00:12:19,937
The cost is that associations between facts are lost entirely—"Works as a Senior Engineer at TechCorp, responsible for recommendation system development" is decomposed into three independent facts ("Works at TechCorp," "Job title is Senior Engineer," "Responsible for a recommendation system"), severing the internal connections within a single job.

85
00:12:19,937 --> 00:12:27,674
When handling queries that require synthesizing multiple pieces of information, the system has to piece the fragments back together.

86
00:12:27,844 --> 00:12:34,981
Enhanced Notes adopts a holistic perspective, saving each memory as a paragraph containing complete context.

87
00:12:34,931 --> 00:12:48,781
For example, the same job information is stored as: "The user has been a Senior Software Engineer at TechCorp, specializing in machine learning for three years, currently leading a recommendation system project with a team of five.

88
00:12:48,781 --> 00:12:53,269
Preserving the narrative structure keeps the semantics complete and rich.

89
00:12:53,269 --> 00:13:03,931
The tradeoffs are storage redundancy (the same information repeated across paragraphs) and update complexity (one attribute change means rewriting several paragraphs).

90
00:13:03,931 --> 00:13:17,156
JSON Cards adopts a three-level nested structure (Category → Subcategory → Key-Value Pair, e.g., personal.contact.email, work.position.title), mimicking the way humans categorize.

91
00:13:17,156 --> 00:13:26,219
It supports partial updates (modifying work.position.title does not affect work.company.name) and is predictable and extensible.

92
00:13:26,219 --> 00:13:42,194
But the rigid structure assumes information can be cleanly categorized—"Developing personal projects in Python on weekends" is at once a time preference, a technical preference, and an activity type; forcing it into a single category flattens those dimensions away.

93
00:13:42,194 --> 00:13:48,831
Advanced JSON Cards represents a shift in memory systems from information storage to knowledge management.

94
00:13:48,831 --> 00:14:01,906
Each card records not only facts but also the narrative context (backstory) of the information source, the subject's identity (person), the relationship with the user (relationship), and a timestamp.

95
00:14:01,906 --> 00:14:17,006
The core idea is that the same piece of information can have completely different meanings in different contexts—"Dr. Zhang" could be the user's own dentist or the user's father's cardiologist; stripped of its context, the information cannot be understood correctly.

96
00:14:17,006 --> 00:14:21,706
This design solves the disambiguation problem of traditional systems.

97
00:14:21,706 --> 00:14:33,569
In real-world scenarios, a user may have information tied to multiple identities (their own, their parents', and their children's), and simple key-value storage cannot accurately distinguish them.

98
00:14:33,569 --> 00:14:49,456
Advanced JSON Cards provide the context in which the information was acquired (the "why" for storing this information) through backstory, and establish a clear entity model (the "for whom" the information is stored) through the person and relationship fields.

99
00:14:49,456 --> 00:14:59,681
When the user says "Help me arrange annual checkups for my family," the system can identify all family members through relationship and understand health history through backstory.

100
00:14:59,681 --> 00:15:03,506
The cost is higher generation and maintenance overhead.

101
00:15:03,506 --> 00:15:20,506
The practical selection criterion is: use Advanced JSON Cards for critical, low-volume data (e.g., user preferences, key personal relationships) to ensure retrievability; use Simple Notes for large volumes of non-critical conversational facts to reduce cost.

102
00:15:20,506 --> 00:15:27,994
Most production systems adopt a hybrid approach—different types of information within the same Agent follow different paths.

103
00:15:28,156 --> 00:15:34,743
Experiment 3-2 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Comparing Memory Strategies

104
00:15:34,693 --> 00:15:41,056
The user-memory project implements the four memory modes described above under a unified interface.

105
00:15:41,056 --> 00:15:52,268
Each mode provides a complete implementation of memory generation (analyzing sessions, writing memories) and memory retrieval (fetching relevant memories based on the current question).

106
00:15:52,268 --> 00:16:08,706
By switching modes at runtime via configuration, you can test each one on the three-level evaluation set from Experiment 3-1: observe the memory representations extracted from the same set of test sessions under different storage formats, and compare the final answer scores.

107
00:16:08,706 --> 00:16:27,031
The experimental observations align with the earlier analysis: Simple Notes passes most "basic recall" cases at the lowest generation cost, but frequently loses points on second- and third-level cases that require synthesizing multiple pieces of information or distinguishing entities with the same name.

108
00:16:27,031 --> 00:16:39,081
Advanced JSON Cards performs best on cases involving disambiguation and cross-session association, at the cost of significantly more expensive and slower memory maintenance calls after each session.

109
00:16:39,081 --> 00:16:52,068
Readers are encouraged to switch between the four modes manually and compare the memory files generated for the same test case—with concrete examples in front of you, the differences between the formats are obvious at a glance.

110
00:16:52,068 --> 00:16:56,181
Advanced Knowledge Representation: Executable Code.

111
00:16:56,181 --> 00:17:08,281
The four formats above are fundamentally text: good at recalling a single fact, but leaving aggregation, contradiction detection, and constraint enforcement to the LLM's "mental arithmetic.

112
00:17:08,281 --> 00:17:19,668
User as Code turns user state into typed, executable objects and writes the rules as ordinary functions, so that "representation" and "reasoning" share one verifiable medium.

113
00:17:19,668 --> 00:17:31,168
It borrows the write-ahead log plus checkpoint mechanism: after a session ends, facts are first appended to an append-only log, and the typed state is periodically rebuilt from the complete log.

114
00:17:31,168 --> 00:17:36,668
This preserves the raw evidence while yielding a queryable, executable derived state.

115
